    <enumeration name="HostingMode" c:type="GjsifyWebView2HostingMode">
      <doc xml:space="preserve"
           filename="src/c/gjsify-webview2.h"
           line="101">What a #GjsifyWebView2WebView actually is on screen. This exists because GTK's
failure mode here is exit 0: a rounded clip that does nothing is
indistinguishable from an application bug forever, so the answer has a name
and a reader.</doc>
      <source-position filename="src/c/gjsify-webview2.h" line="119"/>
      <member name="overlay"
              value="0"
              c:identifier="GJSIFY_WEBVIEW2_HOSTING_MODE_OVERLAY">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="src/c/gjsify-webview2.h"
             line="103">the web content is a child `HWND` the
  OS composites ABOVE the GTK surface. It is not in GSK's scene graph: it
  cannot be clipped by an ancestor, nothing can be drawn over it, and its
  opacity and transforms are not applied.</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="composited"
              value="1"
              c:identifier="GJSIFY_WEBVIEW2_HOSTING_MODE_COMPOSITED">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="src/c/gjsify-webview2.h"
             line="107">the web content arrives as a
  #GdkTexture the widget paints in its own `snapshot` vfunc, so it behaves
  like any other widget. Not produced by this build — ADR 0035 stage 2.</doc>
      </member>
    </enumeration>
